Chapter 27 – Part 1
The suite had gone quieter after the roof. Cake boxes on the table. Chain on the door. Curtis’s phone face-down on the bar like it might start another war if he looked at it.
Mika sat on the far bed with her cap off now, white-blonde hair a little flattened from the brim, and watched him the way she had watched the bottles.
“So. Do you play instruments often?”
Curtis blinked.
“Huh? Oh.” Long black braids shifted as he caught up. “Well. I used to play in a band in Los Angeles. We would play talent shows. Battle of the bands. Do small concerts.”
Mika’s eyebrows went up.
“…You.” Not mean. Just rearranging him again. “The guy who drinks water at a hotel bar and shows people his missing-kid poster also did battle of the bands.”
A small, disbelieving smile.
“That actually explains the singing. And the way you stand like you’re waiting for a count-in even when there’s no music.” She tipped her head. “What did you play? Don’t say everything like a show-off. Pick one.”
Then, softer, because the night had already been too many secrets:
“Were they good nights? The concerts. Or is that another thing you laugh at so you don’t have to miss it.”
On the fifth floor, the hunt stayed outside.
Inside, Mika Harima reached for the version of him that had a stage instead of a scar —
and Curtis Nightstar had to decide how much of Los Angeles a girl named Mika was allowed to hear before morning.
Curtis chuckled.
“Don’t say everything, huh.” Long black braids shifted as he leaned a hip against the bar. “Depends on the song I play. I can play guitar, bass, violin, drums.” A smaller, almost shy beat. “And my personal favorite. Piano.”
Mika stared at him.
“…You just said everything.”
She threw a pillow at his midsection. It bounced off like he had been built for worse.
“Guitar and bass and violin and drums and piano. That’s not a band kid. That’s a whole music department with legs.” The smile stayed, curious now instead of only teasing. “Piano as the favorite is the part I didn’t expect. You look like you’d pick something you can carry when you run.”
A short breath.
“Los Angeles. Talent shows. All those instruments.” She hugged the remaining pillow. “Did you ever play just for someone? Not a crowd. Not a battle. Just a room.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
On the fifth floor, the missing girl had found another door in him —
and it opened on keys instead of knives.
Curtis sighed.
“My ex….”
The word sat in the suite like a glass set down too hard.
He did not decorate it. He did not say cruel or California or she kicked my heart in the ass. The sigh had already done the work. Piano. A room. Someone who had gotten the private version of the songs before she used it.
Mika’s teasing thinned.
“…Ah.” She pulled the pillow in tighter. “That’s the just a room answer.”
A short, careful beat.
“You don’t have to tell me her name. Or what she did. I already know that face.” She glanced at her own reflection in the dark window — reconstructed, borrowed, still learning how to belong to itself. “Someone got the good nights and then made you sorry you handed them over.”
She did not reach for him.
“For what it’s worth… I’m asking about piano. Not about her. If those are the same story tonight, we can talk about drums instead.”
Curtis huffed a quiet, tired sound.
On the fifth floor, Los Angeles arrived as a sigh and a missing name —
and Mika Harima, who had changed her face for a boy, recognized the shape of a song that had been played for the wrong person.
Curtis let the sigh go.
“Hey. How about I play for you.”
He reached into his bag and pulled out a ukulele.
Small. Worn at the neck. Not a stage piano. Not Los Angeles. Just something that fit in a duffle next to clothes and a missing-kid poster and a phone full of other people’s wars.
Mika blinked.
“…You have a ukulele in the emergency bag.”
Then the smile came back, real this time, the one from the dance floor.
“You listed half an orchestra and you pulled out the tiny one.” She scooted back on the far bed and patted the space like a front row that would not ask him for an encore he did not owe. “Yes. Play for me. Not for a crowd. Not for an ex. For the girl in the cap who billed your imaginary parents.”
A smaller beat.
“If it sounds bad I will still clap. I’ve heard worse from boys who thought they were doing me a favor.”
Curtis sat on the edge of the other bed, long black braids falling forward as he tuned by ear, mismatched eyes on the strings instead of her face.
On the fifth floor, the hunt stayed outside.
Inside, a high-roller suite became a room —
and Mika Harima got the private concert she had asked for,
on an instrument small enough to survive a boy who never stayed in one city long.
Curtis chuckled, thumb still on a peg.
“A ukulele is just a small guitar.”
Mika snorted.
“That’s what people say when they don’t want to admit they packed the cute instrument.” She pointed at it with her chin. “Guitars are for sidewalks and fights. That thing is for rooms. You pulled a room instrument after I asked if you ever played for someone.”
A beat, then softer:
“Small guitar. Fine. Play the small guitar, Curtis. I’m already in the front row.”
He huffed, long black braids shifting as he found the last note and let it ring.
On the fifth floor, the argument about size did not matter.
The suite had music in it now —
and Mika Harima was not going to let him hide behind vocabulary when he had already offered her a song.
Curtis rested the ukulele across his knee.
“What do you wanna hear? Upbeat? Sad? A mix.” Long black braids shifted. “Something revealing? Don’t say whatever.”
Mika opened her mouth, shut it, then pointed at him like he had caught her.
“…Rude. That was exactly what I was going to say.”
She thought about the dance floor. The poster. The sigh that had a California girl in it. Morning coming with Mikado’s address on it.
“A mix,” she decided. “Not sad enough that this room turns into a funeral. Not upbeat enough that we pretend nothing happened today.” A smaller, braver beat. “And if there’s a revealing part… keep it in the song. Don’t explain it after. I want to hear you, not a lecture about you.”
She hugged the pillow.
“Play like the suite is the only place that exists. That’s the request. Don’t say you can’t. You already offered.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
“Mix. Room only. Got it.”
On the fifth floor, the first note waited under his fingers —
and Mika Harima leaned in for a concert that was allowed to be honest
without making either of them bleed on the carpet.
Curtis played.
The ukulele was too small for the story in his throat. He sang anyway — growing-up-broke, no phone, hungry, a four-year-old listening to a mother yell through the wall. Used shoes. Hiding behind tunes. Falling and calling it his own fault even when it was an accident.
Long black braids hung forward. Mismatched eyes stayed on the strings.
The middle of the song did not stay polite. Better days. A moment that sucked. The ugly chorus of wanting to quit and do anything else because nobody gave a damn. Outcast. Too shy to talk about home. Life supposed to be cold. A window in the head like a casket. Wishing he was gone and masking it.
Mika stopped breathing on that line.
He did not look up.
The last turn was a hand at a throat and a prayer not to let go, then the same overwhelmed loop again, like the song could not find a door that was not the one he grew up inside.
The last chord rang in the suite and died.
Curtis sat with the ukulele on his knee and did not explain it.
Mika’s fingers were locked in the pillow.
“…You weren’t kidding about revealing.” Her voice came out thin. “Four. Used shoes. Blaming yourself for falling. That’s not a metaphor. That’s the poster with the sound on.”
She swallowed.
“You said don’t make me explain it after. I won’t ask you to. I just—” A short, raw breath. “I asked for a room song. You gave me the house you got thrown out of.”
She did not call him a freak. She did not look at him the way Anri had after the street fight.
“I’m still in the front row,” she said. “I’m not leaving the bed because the small guitar told the truth. If you need a minute, take it. If you want to put that thing away, I’ll still be here.”
A smaller, unsteady beat.
“Thank you. For not pretending it was just a mix.”
On the fifth floor, the concert ended the way she had asked —
no lecture after,
only a missing girl and a missing-poster boy sitting in the quiet
with a song that had just admitted the worst years out loud.
Curtis chuckled, the sound rough after the last chord.
“My bad.”
He reset his hands on the ukulele like he could change the weather in the suite by changing the key.
This one was brighter on the surface — long drives in the back seat, a night the world caught fire, eyes lighting a dark scene. You told me I would be fine. I called you a liar. FaceTime. Falling out of a life. Missing the pain of a heartbreak because ordinary days did not hit the same.
Mika listened with the pillow still in her lap.
Mid-song he flicked his wrist.
A rose assembled in the empty air beside the strings — stem, thorns, bloom — and the red went out of it as if someone had drained a photograph. Green. Whole. Impossible.
He kept playing like he had not just broken a rule of the room.
Sea. Dismal dreams. Falling stars setting a world on fire. Flames that were not meant for two people. Make-believe. Roses turning green. Seven seas pouring into a dream and drowning in it. I wish you weren’t so far from me.
The last turn was almost shy even with the grin he was wearing. Thinking about someone all day. Sun on a face. I think I fell for you. I hate that that’s the truth. Okay. Strange. In pain. Please don’t drive away from me.
The green rose stayed hovering a second longer, then settled into his palm as the last note died.
Curtis held it out, not quite looking at her.
“…Mix,” he said. “Like you asked.”
Mika stared at the flower that should not have existed.
Then at him.
“…You don’t get to say my bad after the first song and then pull a rose out of the air during the second.” Her voice had gone high and thin and alive. “That’s not a small guitar trick. That’s—”
She stopped herself before Dullahan or Yagiri or Saika could make it a lab again.
“That’s you.”
She did not take the rose immediately. She looked at it like it might vanish if she was greedy.
“Upbeat on the outside. Still revealing. You sang about someone being far and then you made the lyric sit in your hand.” A shaky breath. “If that’s for the room and not a promise, say so. Because that song is not casual, Curtis. And neither is a green rose.”
On the fifth floor, the concert had a color now.
Mika Harima sat in front of a boy who could hide a missing-child poster in a phone and a miracle in a wrist flick —
and the suite, for one held note, smelled like a garden that did not belong in Ikebukuro.
Curtis flicked his wrist again.
The green ran out of the petals like a thought changing its mind. Pink settled in — softer, less like a lyric, more like something you could actually hold without asking what it meant.
He held it out.
“Here. You can have this.”
Mika looked at the rose, then at his face, then at the rose again.
“…You’re just going to keep editing it until I take it.”
She took it.
The stem was real in her fingers. Cool. Alive. Not a hologram and not a lab trick she recognized. She brought it close enough to confirm it had a smell, then let it rest against the pillow like it might bruise if she gripped too hard.
“Pink is easier than green,” she said quietly. “Green was the song. Pink is… you handing me something after you scared me on purpose.”
A small, crooked smile.
“Thank you. I’m keeping it. If it dies by morning I’m blaming your magic, not the hotel air.” She glanced at him over the petals. “And I’m still not asking how you did that. Not tonight. You already gave the room two songs and a flower. That’s enough revealing for one suite.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
On the fifth floor, Mika Harima had a pink rose that had been three colors in one night —
and she held it like a person, not like a specimen,
which was the only review that mattered.
Curtis rubbed the back of his neck, long black braids shifting.
“Well. You asked for an upbeat song. So… I don’t know. I thought it would be a good choice…”
Mika looked at him over the pink rose.
“…It was upbeat the way a fire is warm,” she said. “Pretty. Still dangerous if you stand too close.”
She spun the stem once between her fingers.
“The first song was the house. The second one was a drive you can’t sit in the back of. And then you made a rose and turned it pink like that would make the lyrics less loud.” Not accusing. Just accurate. “It was a good choice. I’m not mad. I’m just not going to pretend I think I fell for you is the same category as a drum fill.”
A smaller beat, gentler.
“You tried to lift the room after you dropped it. That counts. Next time you can pick something that doesn’t also confess on accident.” She tipped the rose toward him. “Or you can. I’m not your manager. I’m the girl in the front row with a flower that used to be green.”
Curtis huffed, almost a laugh.
On the fifth floor, the set list had been reviewed —
upbeat enough, revealing anyway,
and Mika Harima was going to keep both songs without making him take the second one back.
Curtis stretched until his shoulders popped, shrugged the blue blazer off, and laid it over the chair like the night could hang there without him for a minute.
He dropped onto his bed. Long black braids fanned against the hotel white. Phone unlocked.
A thread he had been avoiding sat at the top.
