He locked the screen before the next bubble could arrive.
Mika looked over.
Then she crossed the gap between the beds and sat on his mattress, not quite touching, pink rose still in her hand like a ticket she had decided to keep.
Curtis turned his head on the pillow.
“You okay?”
Mika glanced at the dark phone, then at him.
“…Yeah. I just didn’t want to sit on the far bed like I’m still a package.” A short beat. “You went somewhere else the second that name showed up. Rio. I’m not asking who she is if you don’t want. I just—” She shrugged one shoulder. “After those songs, sitting across the room felt stupid.”
She picked at a petal.
“If you need the bed to yourself, say so. I’ll move. I’m not trying to make your night harder than it already is.”
Curtis watched her sit in the space he had left open.
On the fifth floor, Rio had a rain check.
Mika Harima had the edge of his bed.
And you okay had to cover both of them without either girl hearing the other.
Curtis chuckled.
“Classmate. She sits behind Anri.”
Mika’s eyebrow went up.
“So. Friends with two girls.”
Curtis thought about it. The hallway. The lunch table. The shoes. The night Rio had come to the flat. The night Anri had sat on this same kind of quiet.
“You’re not wrong on how it looks.”
He stared at the ceiling instead of the rose.
“I tried the other direction too. Mikado. Masaomi. Regular guy friends. It just—” He flicked his fingers like smoke that wasn’t there. “They shun me for my attitude. Too blunt. Too much. I sleep through class and then I don’t smile the right way when something ugly happens.”
A shorter beat.
“There’s another group. Van gang. Good people, actually. They asked for help and I gave it. Then they spent the whole day calling me a problem while I was standing in their van.” He huffed. “So yeah. Two girls who will sit near me. Boys who flinch. A van that wants the work and not the person. That’s the friend report.”
Mika looked down at the pink rose, then at him.
“…That’s a lonely way to say you’re popular.” Not cruel. Just accurate. “Two girls isn’t a harem if the rest of the city keeps putting you in the problem drawer.”
She stayed on the edge of his bed.
“Anri. Rio. And me on a hotel mattress with a flower you made.” A thin smile. “You’re allowed to have more than one person, Curtis. You’re also allowed to notice that the ones who stay are the ones you didn’t treat like a problem first.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged, tired.
On the fifth floor, the friend list was shorter than the city thought —
and Mika Harima, who had been a tool and a face and a runaway, recognized the shape of a boy everyone used
and almost nobody sat beside.
Curtis turned his head on the pillow.
“That’s bold of you to say, Mika. But thank you. It’s better than being called a problem. Heh.”
Mika’s mouth twitched.
“Bold is just what happens when you already died once and came back with someone else’s cheekbones. I don’t have time to be polite about furniture.”
She set the pink rose on the nightstand between them, not quite giving it back.
“You’re not a problem. You’re loud, and you scare people who wanted a smaller story. That’s different.” A short beat. “Don’t thank me like I did you a favor. I sat down because the far bed was stupid. You can still tell me to move.”
Curtis huffed that small laugh again.
“Noted.”
On the fifth floor, problem got an argument and lost.
Mika Harima stayed on his mattress anyway,
as if the correction needed a body to hold it in place.
Mika watched his face like the answer mattered more than the mattress.
“So. Are you going to tell me to move?”
Curtis shook his head once against the pillow.
“Only if you want to. If I had a problem I would begin with that. But I don’t mind, Mika.” Long black braids shifted. “Whatever keeps you like this. I prefer you not scared.”
The suite went quiet enough to hear the city under the windows.
Mika’s shoulders dropped a fraction she had been holding since the club.
“…Okay.” Not a flirt. A landing. “Then I’m staying here. Not because I’m claiming the bed. Because scared is how I walked into that building, and this—” she gestured at the small space between them, the rose, the dark phone “—is the first time tonight I don’t feel like I’m about to get put back in a drawer.”
She lay back on her side of his mattress, cap on the nightstand, pink rose between them like a border that was also a gift.
“If I kick you in my sleep, that’s a reconstruction problem. Not a confession.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
“Fair.”
On the fifth floor, the far bed stayed empty on purpose.
Mika Harima chose the closer quiet.
And Curtis Nightstar, who collected people without meaning to, let her keep the version of herself that was not afraid.
Then it clicked.
Sleep beside him.
Not sit.
Not talk until morning.
Sleep.
Curtis Nightstar, who had faced Shizuo, a Dullahan’s scythe, and a cartel, fell out of a hotel bed like the floor had been waiting for him.
Thump.
Long black braids spilled over the carpet. Mismatched eyes stared at the underside of the mattress as if it had betrayed him.
Mika leaned over the edge, pink rose still in frame.
“…Did you just now hear the sentence.”
Curtis, from the floor: “You said stay. I heard stay. Stay is sit. Stay is talk. Stay is—”
“Sleep,” Mika finished, not unkind. “I did say that.”
She did not laugh at him the way a cruel person would. She laughed the small, incredulous laugh of a girl who had watched a monster-killer get defeated by a bedspread.
“You told me to do whatever kept me not-scared. This is that. I’m not stripping. I’m not claiming you. I’m a person who doesn’t want the far bed.” A beat. “If the floor is more comfortable for your dignity, I can throw you a pillow.”
Curtis sat up, ears warm, blue blazer still on the chair like it was judging him.
“I’m getting back in the bed.”
“Good. The carpet didn’t pay for the suite.”
He climbed back onto his side like the mattress might explode. Mika stayed on hers, leaving the rose and a respectful strip of hotel white between them.
On the fifth floor, the missing girl had chosen not to be afraid —
and Curtis Nightstar had learned, again, that kindness could knock him down faster than a 2x4
Curtis lay very still on his side of the mattress, staring at the hotel ceiling like it might give him a briefing.
Okay. Well. I can handle that.
Sleep. Clothes on. A rose on the nightstand. A girl who had asked not to be a package.
If she did what Celty did…
The thought arrived uninvited. Nightwear. A pull under the covers. Smoke where a head should be. A body that had felt like Appalachia for one stupid, honest second.
Celty. I miss you.
He did not say it out loud. Mika was already breathing on the other side of the white strip. He would not put a Dullahan’s name into a suite that was keeping her safe.

Elsewhere in Ikebukuro, a helmet sat on a table that still smelled faintly of someone else’s perfume.
Celty Sturluson stood in the dark of a room that was supposed to be home and felt the walls sit wrong.
Curtis…
The PDA stayed dark in her hand. The sentence did not need a screen.
Where are you.
Shinra’s laugh still lived in the other room. Mei’s voice under it. Courier jobs she could take. Streets she already knew.
None of that was the missing piece.
I need you.
She did not send it. Need was a door she had told him not to bother, then walked through anyway, then found locked when the city got loud.
On the fifth floor, Curtis missed a woman with no face.
Across the ward, Celty needed a boy who would not tell her where he was.
The night held both sentences
and delivered neither.
Curtis settled back in.
He draped an arm over his face like the ceiling had gotten too bright, even with the lights low. Long black braids spilled across the pillow. The strip of hotel white between them lasted about ten seconds.
Mika rolled over.
Her arm came across his chest — not grabbing, not claiming, just landing there the way a person landed when they had decided the far side of the bed was a lie. Warm. Light. Cap off. Pink rose still on the nightstand like a witness.
Curtis went still under it.
Okay.
Still handleable.
Still not what Celty did.
He did not move her. He did not make a joke. He breathed out slowly so his ribs would not startle the weight.
Mika’s voice came muffled against the pillow, already halfway under.
“…You’re warm. Don’t make it weird.”
“Wasn’t going to.”
His arm stayed over his eyes. Hers stayed over his chest.
On the fifth floor, two people who had been thrown away by families they could not keep slept in a suite neither of them had paid for with ordinary money —
and for a few hours, Ikebukuro could hunt the street
while the missing girl used a wanderer’s heartbeat as proof she was still here.
Curtis’s voice came from under his arm, quiet enough not to scare the suite.
“You know when you say don’t make it weird it makes it weird.” A small breath. “Kidding. As long as you’re comfortable, Mika.”
Mika’s arm stayed where it was.
“…Shut up. That’s a rule too.” The words were already sleepy. “I’m comfortable. If I wasn’t I’d be on the floor with you from earlier.”
A beat.
“Thanks. For not making me move. For the songs. For the rose.” Her fingers curled once in the fabric of his shirt and then went loose. “Wake me if the city finds us. Otherwise I’m stealing your chest until morning.”
Curtis huffed.
“Got it.”
The hunt stayed outside.
On the fifth floor, don’t make it weird lost to a missing girl falling asleep on a boy who had promised her she did not have to be scared —
and Curtis Nightstar kept still on purpose,
the way you keep still when something rare has finally landed and you do not want to startle it off.